    <!-- ═══ 05 · NO TWO PEOPLE NEED THE SAME THING ═══ -->
    <section class="ctc-dark relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 pointer-events-none" style="background: radial-gradient(120% 90% at 50% 0%, hsl(42 40% 30% / 0.1), transparent 55%);"></div>
      <div class="relative container mx-auto px-6 py-20 md:py-28 max-w-3xl ctc-reveal">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-gold-gradient text-3xl md:text-4xl lg:text-[44px] leading-tight mb-10">No two people need the same thing</h2>

        <div class="font-body text-lg leading-relaxed space-y-7 max-w-[65ch]">
          <p>The same approach will not serve every person, and even for one person, what helps at one stage of life can be quite wrong at another.</p>

          <p>Sometimes the work is practical. There is a decision to make, a boundary to set, a pattern to interrupt before it runs its usual course. Sometimes it is slower, because something painful from the past still shapes relationships and self-worth even when you understand where it came from. Sometimes what is needed is more awareness of emotion or of the body, or learning to stay with discomfort rather than escaping it. Sometimes it is grief, or identity, or the question of what kind of life you actually want to build.</p>

          <p class="ctc-emph">Interventions should serve the person, rather than requiring the person to fit the intervention.</p>

          <p>So the work is integrative and responsive. Psychological reflection, behavioural experiments, mindfulness, attention training, body awareness, journalling or breath practices may each be useful in the right circumstances. They are tools rather than doctrines, and no interest in contemplative practice is required for any of them. The question is always the same: what would actually be useful here, for this person, now?</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ═══ 06 · FROM INSIGHT TO LIVED CHANGE ═══ -->
    <section class="bg-background">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-6 py-20 md:py-28 max-w-3xl ctc-reveal">
        <h2 class="font-heading ctc-gold-text text-3xl md:text-4xl lg:text-[44px] leading-tight mb-10">From insight to lived change</h2>

        <div class="font-body text-lg text-foreground/78 leading-relaxed space-y-7 max-w-[65ch]">
          <p>Understanding yourself matters, but insight on its own has surprisingly little power in the moments where it would count most. Someone may know perfectly well that they avoid conflict, become defensive when they feel exposed, procrastinate when something matters, or lose themselves in other people's needs. The difficulty is rarely recognising the pattern. It is finding what allows a different response while the old one is already unfolding.</p>

          <p>So much of the work stays close to lived experience. We look at what tends to happen just before a familiar reaction, what you feel in the body, which assumptions arrive almost automatically, and what the response costs you afterwards. The purpose is not to reduce everything to a theory, but to understand a pattern precisely enough that there is somewhere practical to intervene.</p>

          <p>That intervention is often very small: having one conversation differently, noticing the point at which anxiety turns into avoidance, setting a boundary before resentment builds, or testing a belief that has quietly governed your behaviour for years. What happens outside the room then becomes part of the next conversation. Over time the process becomes less about collecting insights and more about turning understanding into freedom of action.</p>
        </div>

        <blockquote class="mt-12 md:mt-14 border-l-2 border-primary/50 pl-7 md:pl-8">
          <p class="font-heading ctc-gold-text text-[26px] md:text-[32px] italic leading-snug max-w-[34ch]">The measure of this work is not how someone feels during a session, but how they function in the days and weeks that follow.</p>
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

